---
import { VERSION } from '../consts'
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro'
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro'

/*
 * Written because the Microsoft Store requires a privacy policy URL, and every
 * other store asks for one eventually. But a policy written to satisfy a form
 * is the kind nobody reads, so this one answers the question a person actually
 * has: what leaves my machine, and who can see it.
 *
 * The honest answer is uncomfortable, and it is the reason this page leads with
 * the swarm rather than burying it under a cookie paragraph. rigseed collects
 * nothing at all, which would make for a very short and very smug page; the
 * part that matters to a reader is that BitTorrent itself is public, and that
 * is true of every client, not a rigseed failing. Saying so plainly is worth
 * more than a page that technically discloses it in clause nine.
 *
 * Every claim here is checkable against the source, which is why the page keeps
 * naming files and endpoints instead of saying "certain information".
 */

const repo = 'https://github.com/verastack-labs/rigseed-app'
const updated = 'October 2026'
---

<Base
  title="rigseed privacy"
  description="What rigseed sends, what it stores, and who can see your activity. No telemetry in the app, and an honest answer about what BitTorrent reveals."
  version={VERSION}
  releaseUrl={`${repo}/releases/latest`}
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="wrap page">
    <header class="head">
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The rigseed app has no analytics, no telemetry, no accounts and no servers of its own. This
        website counts page views, and says so below. The interesting part of this page is still not
        what rigseed collects, which is nothing, but what BitTorrent itself reveals, which is a
        great deal.
      </p>
      <p class="updated mono">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </header>

    <section class="band">
      <h2>The part that actually matters</h2>
      <p>
        When you download or seed a torrent, your IP address is visible to every other peer in that
        swarm, to the trackers coordinating it, and to anyone running the distributed hash table
        nodes your client talks to. This is not a flaw and it is not specific to rigseed. It is how
        BitTorrent works: peers connect directly to each other, so each one necessarily learns the
        other's address.
      </p>
      <p>
        That means third parties can and do record which torrents an address participated in.
        Anti-piracy monitors join swarms for exactly this purpose. rigseed cannot change this, and
        no torrent client can. If that matters for your situation, the protection has to come from
        how you connect, not from which client you choose.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What rigseed sends</h2>
      <p>Three things leave your machine, and only three.</p>

      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>Update checks</dt>
        <dd>
          rigseed asks GitHub whether a newer release exists, by requesting
          <code class="mono">{repo}/releases/latest/download/latest.json</code>. GitHub sees your IP
          address and user agent, as it would for any file you downloaded from it. The response is
          verified against a signing key compiled into the app, so a tampered reply is rejected
          rather than installed. Builds installed from a store do not make this request at all,
          because the store handles updates.
        </dd>

        <dt>Torrent traffic</dt>
        <dd>
          Everything described above: trackers, peers and DHT nodes, handled by the bundled
          <code class="mono">qbittorrent-nox</code>. This only happens for torrents you add.
        </dd>

        <dt>Search plugins, if you use them</dt>
        <dd>
          qBittorrent's search feature fetches plugins from
          <code class="mono">plugins.qbittorrent.org</code> and queries the sites those plugins target.
          It is off until you enable it.
        </dd>
      </dl>

      <p class="note">
        There is no fourth thing. No crash reporter, no usage statistics, no &ldquo;anonymous&rdquo;
        identifiers, no first-run ping. You can check this: the app&rsquo;s source is public and
        searching it for the usual analytics names returns nothing. That is the app. This website is
        a separate thing and counts page views, which is further down.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What rigseed stores, and where</h2>
      <p>
        Your settings, your torrent list and qBittorrent's own configuration are written to your
        computer and stay there. Nothing is synced, backed up or uploaded anywhere.
      </p>
      <ul class="paths">
        <li><code class="mono">%APPDATA%\dev.verastack.rigseed</code> on Windows</li>
        <li>
          <code class="mono">~/Library/Application Support/dev.verastack.rigseed</code> on macOS
        </li>
        <li><code class="mono">~/.local/share/dev.verastack.rigseed</code> on Linux</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Deleting that directory removes everything rigseed remembers. Uninstalling does not touch
        the files you downloaded, which live wherever you told it to put them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        These pages are static files on GitHub Pages. There are no cookies. GitHub, as the host,
        logs requests including IP addresses; that is covered by
        <a
          href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-privacy-statement"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank">GitHub's privacy statement</a
        >. Fonts are served from these pages rather than from a font provider.
      </p>
      <p>
        One script is loaded from elsewhere: <strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong>, from
        <code class="mono">static.cloudflareinsights.com</code>. It counts page views so we can tell
        whether anyone is reading this. It sets no cookies, stores nothing on your machine and
        builds no profile that follows you to other sites, which is why there is no consent banner
        to click past. It does mean Cloudflare sees that a request for this page was made, and what
        it collects is covered by
        <a
          href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics-privacy/"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank">their privacy summary for the product</a
        >. Blocking it changes nothing about how the site works.
      </p>
      <p>
        The changelog is built from the release notes when the site is built, not when you visit it,
        so reading it does not contact GitHub on your behalf.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>If you installed from a store</h2>
      <p>
        Stores run their own collection, which rigseed has no part in and no visibility into. If you
        installed from one, its policy applies alongside this one: the
        <a
          href="https://privacy.microsoft.com/privacystatement"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank">Microsoft privacy statement</a
        >
        for the Microsoft Store, or
        <a href="https://flathub.org/terms-and-conditions" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"
          >Flathub's terms</a
        > for Flatpak.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Your data, and asking about it</h2>
      <p>
        Because rigseed holds nothing about you, there is nothing to export, correct or delete on
        request; the data is already entirely in your hands. If you want to raise something, open an
        issue on
        <a href={`${repo}/issues`} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">the tracker</a> or write
        to <a href="mailto:rigan@verastack.dev">rigan@verastack.dev</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the updated date above changes with it, and the change goes through
        the public repository like everything else, so the history is readable rather than
        announced.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .head {
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .lede {
    margin-top: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .updated {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-dimmer);
  }

  section {
    margin-top: clamp(2.25rem, 5vw, 3.25rem);
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  section h2 {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  section p {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    margin: 0 0 0.875rem;
  }

  /*
   * The swarm paragraph is the one thing on this page a reader would regret
   * skimming, so it is the one thing that does not look like the rest of it.
   */
  .band {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    padding-left: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 1.5rem);
  }

  .band p {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .facts {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
  }

  .facts dt {
    font-weight: 500;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .facts dd {
    margin: 0.375rem 0 0;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  code {
    font-size: 0.8125em;
    color: var(--text);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .paths {
    margin: 0 0 0.875rem;
    padding-left: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }

  .paths li {
    margin-bottom: 0.375rem;
  }

  .note {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  a {
    color: inherit;
  }
</style>
